{
  "version": "1.2.0",
  "date": "2026-10-11",
  "title": "CheckLink look-alike brand list",
  "language": "en-GB",
  "licence": "MIT (Stormberry AS, like the rest of this repository)",
  "about": [
    "The brands whose look-alike addresses CheckLink names (spec section 3, warning 5; stage 3). tools/brands.mjs turns this file into src/brands.js; node tools/brands.mjs --check proves src/brands.js is current and that every domain below has a passing record in rules/brands-verified.json, which node tools/brands.mjs --verify writes from a DNS and HTTPS check run by a person, online.",
    "Who is listed: the providers CheckLink already knows from its tracker rules (rules/provider-rules.json families and the search engines and Steam link filter in rules/extra-rules.json), plus the payment, bank, post and public-service names most often imitated in messages to people in Norway, Portugal and Brazil, and the wallets whose recovery phrases CheckLink's QR inspector hides. group says which; provider names the provider family when there is one.",
    "domains: the brand's own registrable domains (the part of a host name its owner registered, as the Public Suffix List reads it). A host under any of them is never called a look-alike of any brand. A host whose registrable label is exactly one of a brand's marks under an ordinary ending (amazon.co.uk, dnb.com, posten.se) is never called one either: the same name under another ending is often the same company, or a different company with the same name, and CheckLink cannot tell which.",
    "marks: the names CheckLink looks for, each with the domain it cites as the brand's own. kind distinct: a name that hardly ever means anything but this brand, so a longer name that holds it (paypal-community-help.com), a one-letter typing slip (paypall.com) and a name under a free hosting address all count. kind word: an ordinary word or a short name (apple, amazon, posten, dnb), which only counts when written with letters from another alphabet or with digits swapped in, when the brand's own domain is written inside a longer address (apple.com.verify.top), when it sits alone under a free hosting address, or when it is joined to one of the lure words below (dnb-sikkerhet.no, apple-id-verify.com).",
    "platforms: names the brand runs that the Public Suffix List lists as public suffixes because every name under them is a separate site, all of them the brand's own (youtube.googleapis.com, io.withgoogle.com). A host under one is treated like a host under the brand's domains. Platforms where anyone can make a name (github.io, blogspot.com, web.app, a storage bucket under blob.core.windows.net) are deliberately not listed: a brand's name under those is exactly what the free hosting check is for.",
    "probe: for a domain with no page of its own at its bare name (a sign-in or content host), the host that --verify opens instead; it must answer from under one of the brand's domains.",
    "real_names: names that the look-alike checks would otherwise name, each looked up online on the date given (DNS, where HTTPS lands, the page title) and found to be a real name, not a look-alike. words: an ordinary word, or a name several organisations share, one typing slip from a mark (correos, the Spanish word for post office, is one letter from correios); the slip, digit and hyphen checks leave a registrable name that is exactly such a word alone, under any ending, and so does the slip check inside a longer name. domains: one registrable domain that belongs to another organisation (d-nb.info, the German National Library), or to the brand itself where it cannot join the domain list (a name that answers only over plain http); it gets no brand note at all. Add to either list only after looking the name up, and say what was found.",
    "hosted: names under a free hosting address that the brand itself holds, so CheckLink does not call them look-alikes (decision L1 of 11 October 2026). Only GitHub Pages names so far: GitHub gives <account>.github.io to the GitHub account of that name and to no one else, so the name is the brand's for as long as the account is. Each was checked online on the date given (checked): the account exists, it is an organisation that GitHub's domain check shows controls one of the brand's own domains, and it serves the page; found says what was seen, source where. A name freed by a rename or a deleted account can be claimed by someone else, so every name is checked again within a year (recheck_by, at most a year after checked): look the account up again the same way, then update checked, found and recheck_by, or remove the name. node tools/brands.mjs --check fails once a recheck_by date has passed. A host under a hosted name is treated like a host under the brand's domains. The neutral note every address under github.io gets stays on these names too (anyone can make an address under github.io, so the name belongs to whoever made it): it is true for them as well and does not suggest a fake (stage 4b review SC-6, 12 October 2026).",
    "lure_words: words that phishing addresses join to a brand's name; strong ones count for every mark, weak ones only for distinct marks. concat: also counted when written straight onto the name with no hyphen (paypallogin.com)."
  ],
